<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Tamassos</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 343 BCE (approx.) · capital Tamassos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Inland Cypriot kingdom built on rich copper mines, whose last king sold his realm to the king of Kition for fifty talents.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tamassos lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedieos">Pediaios</a> valley at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troodos-mountains">Troodos</a> mountains and was settled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. Its copper may be the 'Temese' of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a>. By the 7th century BCE it was an independent kingdom; its king Admetus appears among the Cypriot rulers listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon of Assyria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom's wealth came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mining and smelting. Its royal tombs of the 6th century BCE are built of fine ashlar masonry imitating wooden houses, and a sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and the Mother of the Gods has yielded much sculpture. Tamassos used Greek and Phoenician alongside each other.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 4th century BCE King Pasicyprus sold Tamassos to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pumiyaton">Pumiyaton</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kition">Kingdom of Kition</a> for fifty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> later gave it to the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamis-cyprus">Salamis</a>. Tamassos survived as a town under Ptolemaic and Roman rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1000 BCE – 343 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy